---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="TableRead Tutorials — DataIsland Software"
  description="Step-by-step guides for TableRead — casting characters, importing voice models, generating full or partial AI read-throughs, and saving the results."
>

  <div class="tut-wrap">

    <div class="tut-header fade-in">
      <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/tutorials">← All Tutorials</a></div>
      <h1>TableRead — Tutorials</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        Written guides for every feature in TableRead, the casting and AI read-through app in the
        PolySpeak pipeline. Work through them in order for a full introduction, or jump to any
        section using the contents list.
      </p>
      <button class="print-btn" onclick="window.print()">Save as PDF / Print</button>
    </div>

    <div class="tut-layout">

      <!-- Table of contents -->
      <nav class="toc">
        <h2>Contents</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#t1">Opening &amp; creating scripts</a></li>
          <li><a href="#t2">Casting characters</a></li>
          <li><a href="#t3">Importing a voice model directly</a></li>
          <li><a href="#t4">Full read-through vs. a selection</a></li>
          <li><a href="#t5">Playback &amp; saving audio</a></li>
          <li><a href="#t6">Multi-window &amp; saving your session</a></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <!-- Tutorial content -->
      <div class="tut-content">

        <!-- ─── Tutorial 1 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="tut-section" id="t1">
          <h2><span class="num">1</span> Opening &amp; Creating Scripts</h2>
          <p class="goal">What you'll learn: bringing a script into TableRead — with or without PolyScript.</p>

          <ol class="steps">
            <li>Press <strong>⌘O</strong>, or choose <strong>File → Open…</strong>, to open an existing script.</li>
            <li>TableRead opens both <strong>.fountain</strong> and <strong>.fusiondoc</strong> files directly. You don't need PolyScript installed — any properly formatted Fountain screenplay works, from PolyScript or any other source.</li>
            <li>Press <strong>⌘N</strong>, or choose <strong>File → New</strong>, to start a new script from scratch inside TableRead itself.</li>
          </ol>

          <div class="tip">
            <strong>No PolyScript required.</strong> TableRead reads the Fountain format, not a
            PolyScript-specific one — it's a standalone entry point into the PolySpeak pipeline for
            anyone who already has a script, however it was written.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Tutorial 2 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="tut-section" id="t2">
          <h2><span class="num">2</span> Casting Characters</h2>
          <p class="goal">What you'll learn: assigning a trained or imported voice to each character before generating a read-through.</p>

          <ol class="steps">
            <li>Open the character sidebar — TableRead lists every character it finds in the script automatically.</li>
            <li>Click a character to open its casting slot.</li>
            <li>Choose a voice from your Talent library to assign it to that character.</li>
            <li>Repeat for each character you want to cast. Cast characters show as ready in the sidebar; uncast characters are flagged.</li>
          </ol>

          <div class="tip">
            <strong>Clean character names pay off here.</strong> Casting is per character name, so a
            typo'd name in the script (e.g. "SARAH" vs "SARA") shows up as a second, uncast phantom
            character. Fix inconsistent names in the script rather than casting the same voice twice.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Tutorial 3 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="tut-section" id="t3">
          <h2><span class="num">3</span> Importing a Voice Model Directly</h2>
          <p class="goal">What you'll learn: casting a character with a voice model file, without needing Talent at all.</p>

          <ol class="steps">
            <li>In a character's casting slot, choose <strong>Import Voice File…</strong> instead of picking from your library.</li>
            <li>Select an RVC <strong>.pth</strong> model file from disk.</li>
            <li>TableRead assigns it to that character for this session — no trip to Talent required.</li>
          </ol>

          <div class="tip">
            <strong>When to use this.</strong> This is the fastest path if you already have a .pth
            file from elsewhere and just want to hear a scene — you don't need to add it to your
            Talent library first. If you'll reuse the voice across projects, importing it into Talent
            instead keeps its resume and consent record in one place (see the Talent tutorials).
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Tutorial 4 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="tut-section" id="t4">
          <h2><span class="num">4</span> Full Read-Through vs. a Selection</h2>
          <p class="goal">What you'll learn: the two ways to generate audio, and when to reach for each one.</p>

          <h3>Generating a full read-through</h3>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li>Cast every character in the script — the full read-through button stays disabled until all characters have a voice assigned.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Generate Read-Through</strong>. TableRead converts every line of dialogue in the script, in order, through each character's assigned voice.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>Use this once you're ready to hear the entire cast together — a full table read of the script, start to finish.</p>

          <div class="tip">
            <strong>A read-through is a flat reading, not a performance.</strong> TableRead's AI
            voices read the lines as written — useful for testing casting and hearing the whole
            cast together, but it's a mechanical delivery, not a captured performance. It's the
            same relationship film motion capture has to a finished character: an actor covered
            in tracking markers delivers a real physical performance, and that performance drives
            the CG character — the timing, the emotion, the choices are the actor's own, just
            rendered in a different skin. Studio works the same way for voice — a real performer
            delivers the lines with real timing and emotion, and Studio's conversion carries that
            performance through the cast AI voice. TableRead tells you whether the casting works.
            Studio is where you get the actual performance — see the
            <a href="/tutorials/studio">Studio tutorials</a> once casting is settled.
          </div>

          <h3>Generating just a selection</h3>
          <ol class="steps">
            <li>Select the dialogue lines you want to hear — a scene, an exchange, or a single speech.</li>
            <li>Only the characters who speak within that selection need to be cast.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Generate Selection</strong>. TableRead converts just those lines.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Use this to test a single scene or a single character's voice before committing to casting
            everyone — much faster than generating the whole script just to check one line reads well.
          </p>

          <div class="tip">
            <strong>Test small, commit big.</strong> A common workflow is: cast one or two key
            characters, generate a selection of their toughest scene to make sure the voice choice
            works, then finish casting everyone and generate the full read-through once you're happy.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Tutorial 5 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="tut-section" id="t5">
          <h2><span class="num">5</span> Playback &amp; Saving Audio</h2>
          <p class="goal">What you'll learn: listening to a generated read-through and saving it to disk.</p>

          <ol class="steps">
            <li>Once generation finishes, click <strong>Play</strong> to hear the result.</li>
            <li>Click <strong>Save…</strong> to export the generated read-through as a WAV file.</li>
            <li>Choose where to save the file and give it a name — perfect for quick YouTube projects or testing casting before moving to performance capture.</li>
          </ol>

          <div class="tip">
            <strong>Unlimited characters, AI voices included.</strong> TableRead supports unlimited
            characters with no additional cost — ideal for quick YouTube episodes or audio book projects
            where you need a finished track immediately. For performance capture (real emotional delivery
            through trained voices), continue to Studio after you're happy with the casting.
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ─── Tutorial 6 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <section class="tut-section" id="t6">
          <h2><span class="num">6</span> Multi-Window &amp; Saving Your Session</h2>
          <p class="goal">What you'll learn: working on more than one script at once and keeping your casting and read-throughs safe.</p>

          <ol class="steps">
            <li>Each script you open or create in TableRead gets its own window — cast and generate for several scripts side by side.</li>
            <li>Press <strong>⌘S</strong> to save your casting and generated audio references with the script, or <strong>⇧⌘S</strong> to Save As under a new name.</li>
            <li>Dragging a .fountain or .fusiondoc file onto the TableRead icon in the Dock always opens it in TableRead for that one file.</li>
          </ol>

          <div class="tip">
            <strong>Double-clicking opens PolyScript or FusionMD, not TableRead — on purpose.</strong>
            PolyScript owns .fountain and FusionMD owns .fusiondoc as their default editors, so
            double-clicking a script in Finder opens one of those. To open a script in TableRead
            specifically, use <strong>File → Open</strong> inside TableRead, or drag the file onto
            TableRead's Dock icon. If you'd rather make TableRead the default for a file type,
            select a file of that type in Finder, press <strong>⌘I</strong> (Get Info — no
            right-click needed), choose TableRead under <strong>Open with</strong>, and click
            <strong>Change All…</strong>.
          </div>

          <div class="tip">
            <strong>Casting is saved with the script.</strong> When you reopen a saved TableRead
            script, your character-to-voice assignments come back with it — you don't need to
            re-cast every time you revisit a project.
          </div>
        </section>

      </div><!-- /tut-content -->
    </div><!-- /tut-layout -->
  </div><!-- /tut-wrap -->

</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .tut-wrap {
    max-width: 980px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem 5rem;
  }

  .tut-header { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }

  .breadcrumb { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--blue); }

  .tut-header h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }

  .intro {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--gray-dark);
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 640px;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .print-btn {
    background: var(--blue);
    color: #fff;
    border: none;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 0.5rem 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: var(--transition);
  }
  .print-btn:hover { background: #005fa3; }

  .tut-layout {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr;
    gap: 3rem;
    align-items: start;
    animation: layout-enter 1.2s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) 1.0s both;
  }

  @keyframes layout-enter {
    from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(60px); }
    to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
  }

  .toc {
    position: sticky;
    top: 2rem;
    background: var(--gray-light);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 1.25rem 1.25rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .toc h2 {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    color: var(--gray);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .toc ol { list-style: decimal; padding-left: 1.2rem; margin: 0; }
  .toc li { margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  .toc a { font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--gray-dark); text-decoration: none; line-height: 1.4; display: block; }
  .toc a:hover { color: var(--blue); }

  .tut-section {
    padding-bottom: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--gray-light);
  }

  .tut-section:last-child { border-bottom: none; margin-bottom: 0; }

  .tut-section h2 {
    font-size: 1.45rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .num {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 1.8rem;
    height: 1.8rem;
    background: var(--blue);
    color: #fff;
    border-radius: 50%;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .goal {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--gray-dark);
    font-style: italic;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    padding-left: 2.4rem;
  }

  .tut-section h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 0.5rem; color: var(--blue); }
  .tut-section p  { font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }

  .steps { padding-left: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .steps li { font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }

  .tut-section ul  { padding-left: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .tut-section ul li { font-size: 0.97rem; line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    margin: 1rem 0 1.25rem;
  }

  th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    background: var(--gray-light);
    font-weight: 600;
    border: 1px solid #e0e0e0;
  }

  td {
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid #e0e0e0;
    vertical-align: top;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  code {
    font-family: "SF Mono", "Fira Code", monospace;
    font-size: 0.88em;
    background: var(--gray-light);
    padding: 0.1em 0.4em;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  .tip {
    background: var(--blue-light, rgba(0,102,255,0.06));
    border-left: 3px solid var(--blue);
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }

  .lang-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  .lang-row span {
    background: var(--gray-light);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.7rem;
    font-size: 0.84rem;
    color: var(--gray-dark);
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .tut-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .toc { position: static; }
  }

  @media print {
    .print-btn, .breadcrumb { display: none; }
    .tut-layout { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .toc { position: static; page-break-after: always; }
    .tut-section { page-break-inside: avoid; }
    body { font-size: 11pt; }
    h1 { font-size: 22pt; }
    h2 { font-size: 14pt; }
  }
</style>
